so my relationship with Halloween All  Saints Day and the idea of saints in  general is very strange you see I was  raised in a very conservative  conservative and fundamentalist  nomination of Christianity for roughly  18 years of my life pretty much from  birth till I came to Yale I was one of  Jehovah's Witnesses a couple of years  before I was born my parents who were at  the time run of the middle 20-something  Mexican Catholic immigrants took heat of  the gospel being preached at their door  and they converted a couple years later  I was born to boy it'll boil it all down  all I ever knew was life inside this  religion and life as a Jehovah's Witness  kid is tough no birthdays no Boy Scouts  no Christmas no Easter and of course the  Halloween now if you want me to explain  all the theological and doctrinal  intricacies of why the jobs witnesses  choose not to celebrating in any of  these please find me after worship and  I'll gladly explain I'm a really two  studies major I live for this kind of  stuff anyways growing up I was thought  of the Saints of the strange foreign and  definitely Catholic idea that didn't  quite square with how I understood my  relationship with God in Jesus however  as I faded away from the job as  witnesses as I shifted towards  mainstream Christianity and as my faith  matured I began to formulate a more  solid idea of who the Saints were to me  now I don't have all the stuff about how  the saints might be intercessors between  us and God hammered out yet whether or  not the Saints occupy a different space  and this hierarchy of the faithful it's  not really something i'm concerned with  I do however think I've identified some  Saints in my own life this brings me to  this ridiculously oversized stole that  I'm warning today you see this stole  belongs to one of the Saints in my life  some of you might know him his name is  John gage and he preaches right across  the street at United Church on the green  if you've ever met him you know that  John is a big guy barrel chested and  muscle bound as he is John definitely  exudes strength however aside from  physical strength I admire John for a  spiritual strength John I have a couple  things in common we're both foodies with  an affinity for the fries a116 crown  and the mac and cheese of kassius we  both major in religious studies at Yale  albeit with 19 years between our  graduating years but most importantly at  least to me is the fact that we're both  gay men in the Christian faith seeing  lesbian gay bisexual and transgender  people living their lives fully as  Christians is something i never had  growing up and something i didn't know  was possible until i left home and came  to yell seeing people like John lived  her life reminds me of the love God  feels towards his children it reminds me  of the words of the Prophet Jeremiah  where he says how God newest before we  were formed in our mother's womb before  we were born we were set apart as his to  me seeing people like John reinforces my  belief that despite where I came from or  who I was God already loved me and  accepted me despite whatever I was  taught to me that love of God is a big  oversized thing much like this stole  that wraps us up and holds us even  though we don't know it like our Lord  Jesus Christ illustrates in the Gospel  of Luke God wishes to cover us and hold  us like a hand gathering her chicks  under her wings if the saints are meant  to be spiritual role models for us then  John definitely is one for me as I  mature my Christian faith I recently the  Christian faith that I recently  reclaimed I clearly see the need for a  church that has positive Frank and  affirming discussions around where  lesbian gay bisexual and transgender  people fit into the fog my experiences  as a gay Christian led me to become a  religious studies and at queer studies  major it also led me to search out the  university church as an open and  affirming place to live this newfound  faith it also led me to find a saint  like John if you don't I can only hope  that in the future my life leads me to  be to find more Saints like John and  people in this church and God willing  become a saint for someone myself  when i was younger i looked at my  grandma with a sense of amazement we  would drive down to visit her home a  farm west of st. Louis for us it was a  vacation spot for her it was the place  where she had lived since childhood the  land where her grandfather father  husband and son had raised cattle corn  and soybeans and the place she raised  her own family I thought she was so  strong and tough even though i grumbled  when this manifested in rules like no  food not even milk between meals i  watched in wonder as she extinguished  candle flames between her arthritic  thumb and forefinger were walked  tirelessly up and down the rows tending  her expansive garden she had endured  events that i could only imagine or  learn about in history class the Great  Depression a world war a heart attack  and triple bypass surgery several floods  several droughts she had seen an unborn  child parents and later a sister and her  beloved husband died amidst it all she  cared for her family raising five  children in a home that was heated by a  wood-burning stove and did not have  indoor plumbing until my grandma was 50  years old and my mother the youngest of  the five was in high school I had never  seen grandma cry she rarely complained  yet for all of her toughness she was  also the same grandma who rollers in her  hair beckoned my brother sister and me  to sit on her lap for devotions who kept  packs of big red and Doublemint gum in  the back closet for us and who always  opened her home and had homemade meals  ready as I grew I began to realize how  integral my grandma and her strength was  to our family though this strength  deserved wonder it wasn't just a novelty  I began to see how she had given my  grandpa great strength and support how  she nurtured each of her five children  turn each of her 19 grandchildren it  seems there was hardly a trial or  tribulation she hadn't faced through it  all she remains strong leaning on the  one who gave her strength she relied on  the word trusting God and God's plan and  built our family on this foundation  grandma knew well that earthly life was  temporary and fleeting time to bide  until reaching her heavenly home she was  grounded by her faith sure of her  ultimate goal the things of this world  of earthly life were little significance  our family loves baseball and were  diehard Cardinals fans my mom's favorite  player of all time is Hall of Fame left  fielder Lou Brock and she kept a poster  in her old bedroom of Lou sliding into  Cielo base on one of our visits to the  farm however we went up to her room to  discover that the poster of Lou Brock  had been replaced by a painting of jesus  now my mom was not actually upset about  the switch in and of itself but she  wanted to know where the poster had gone  so she could take it home with us my  grandma had no idea the poster was  thrown in the trash or forever lost my  grandma was surprised that my mom was  upset by this and reminded her it's just  earthly things grandma's gaze was always  heavenward over the recent years my  grandma's health  my grandma's health and cognitive  function declined even through that time  though she never feared death instead  she looked forward with joyful  anticipation to the day she would go to  heaven to meet her Lord to be reunited  with her family who had gone before in  may I had the privilege of being with my  grandma during the last moments of her  earthly life my aunts uncles and cousins  had stayed with her throughout the day  and my mom sister brother and I  surrounded her into the night we read  her confirmation verse Isaiah 54 10  though the mountains be shaken and the  hills be removed yet my unfailing love  for you will not be shaken nor my  covenant of peace be removed my mom lay  with my grandma as they had so many  times before and with her hand over my  grandma's chest my mom felt my grandma's  last heartbeat as she left us to receive  new life I apologized to my grandma for  the tears I cried that night and for the  tears yet to come because I know it was  not a sad moment for her I was and am  still working on building my own  strength I am NOT the woman my grandma  was but I am ever thankful that gossip  God saw fit to bless my life was such a  woman who had so much to teach me the  things of this life come and go life  comes and goes but Christ and the  promise of eternal life is constant I'd  like to close by sharing the words of a  letter my grandma wrote to our family  jun 2nd 1989 to my dear children spouses  and grandchildren who knows when our  final hour will come be ready for that  day all your lifetime my one desire and  hope has been that you will cherish the  one thing needful and believe that  wisdoms highest noblest treasure is your  belief in Jesus and his saving grace it  will be heaven if we are all there thank  you for having been good thoughtful  children  and forgive my mistakes and failures as  a mother keep christ in you and he will  help you over the rough places of life  and give you much joy I pray that the  Lord will keep the circle of our family  unbroken when we stand before him on the  day of judgment my life has been a full  and happy one on this earth with  trusting and the Lord this is my wish  for you also your loving mother  mother-in-law and grandmother Alma
 

